import re
ADD = {}

ADD['p1'] = ("1.6 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "1.6 The mouth: Exodus 4"),
("p", "The office has a plain picture at its beginning, before any prophet is named. Moses pleads that he is “slow of speech, and of a slow tongue” (Exodus 4:10). The LORD answers with a question that takes the matter out of Moses’ hands: “Who hath made man’s mouth?” (Exodus 4:11). Then comes the promise: “Now therefore go, and I will be with thy mouth, and teach thee what thou shalt say” (Exodus 4:12). The speaker is sent, the words are taught, and the mouth is the LORD’s to give."),
("p", "When Moses asks again to be excused, the LORD provides Aaron, and the arrangement he describes is the pattern of the whole office in small. “And thou shalt speak unto him, and put words in his mouth: and I will be with thy mouth, and with his mouth, and will teach you what ye shall do” (Exodus 4:15). The word passes from the LORD to Moses, from Moses to Aaron, and from Aaron to the people. “And he shall be thy spokesman unto the people: and he shall be, even he shall be to thee instead of a mouth, and thou shalt be to him instead of God” (Exodus 4:16)."),
("p", "Three things follow, and the volume will hold them through every later chapter. First, the one who speaks is not the author. Aaron is “instead of a mouth,” and a mouth has nothing of its own to say. Second, the words are given beforehand, and the teaching is the LORD’s: “teach thee what thou shalt say.” A prophet does not compose a message and then ask the LORD to bless it. Third, the hearers are not asked to judge Aaron’s eloquence. Moses had said he was not eloquent, and the LORD did not answer that he would become so. The authority of the word did not rest on the quality of the mouth."),
("p", "This is the argument against the claim, still made, that a true prophet is known by the force of his speech or the warmth of his manner. The LORD chose a man who said he could not speak well, and he made a second man to be his mouth. The tests the prophets later give do not examine the speaker’s gifts. They ask where the word came from, where it leads, and whether it stands. Exodus 4 shows why. The mouth was never what made the word true."),
''', "The words are put in the prophet’s mouth, as Exodus 4:12, 15 shows: the one who speaks is the mouth and not the author, and the speaker’s gifts are not the test.")

ADD['p2'] = ("2.6 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "2.6 Micah: full of power by the spirit of the LORD"),
("p", "The charge of Jeremiah 23 is not Jeremiah’s alone. A century earlier Micah describes the same trade and gives its mark. The LORD speaks “concerning the prophets that make my people err, that bite with their teeth, and cry, Peace; and he that putteth not into their mouths, they even prepare war against him” (Micah 3:5). The prophets cry “Peace” to the man who feeds them and prepare war against the man who does not. Their word follows the table. That is as plain a description of a word that comes from the prophet’s own interest as the Scripture gives."),
("p", "The judgment is a silence. “Therefore night shall be unto you, that ye shall not have a vision; and it shall be dark unto you, that ye shall not divine” (Micah 3:6). The seers “shall all cover their lips; for there is no answer of God” (Micah 3:7). The last clause is the point. A prophet is a man to whom God answers, and when the answer stops, the trade has nothing left to sell. The false prophet’s craft was to go on speaking when the LORD had stopped."),
("p", "Micah then puts himself beside them, and what he says of himself is the positive side of the test of source. “But truly I am full of power by the spirit of the LORD, and of judgment, and of might, to declare unto Jacob his transgression, and to Israel his sin” (Micah 3:8). He does not offer a reputation, a school or a sign. He says the word he carries comes by the spirit of the LORD, and he names its content: the transgression of Jacob and the sin of Israel. The true prophet claims a source and takes the consequences of the claim."),
("p", "The volume argues from this that the source of a word shows itself in what the word does to its hearers. A word that exposes “his transgression” and “his sin” is a word that costs the one who carries it, and Micah’s peers prepared war against the man who would not feed them. The word that cries “Peace” is not wrong because it is gentle. It is wrong when the people’s sin is unspoken and the speaker is paid for the silence. Lamentations 2:14 gives the verdict in a single sentence: “Thy prophets have seen vain and foolish things for thee: and they have not discovered thine iniquity, to turn away thy captivity.” A prophecy that does not uncover iniquity cannot turn captivity, and the captivity came."),
''', "Micah 3:5–8 and Lamentations 2:14 give the mark of the paid word: it cries “Peace” and leaves the iniquity unspoken; the true prophet is full of power by the spirit of the LORD to declare the transgression.")

ADD['p3'] = ("3.6 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "3.6 To the law and to the testimony"),
("p", "Isaiah gives the test of loyalty its shortest form in a day when many voices claimed to speak for the dead and the unseen. “And when they shall say unto you, Seek unto them that have familiar spirits, and unto wizards that peep, and that mutter: should not a people seek unto their God? for the living to the dead?” (Isaiah 8:19). Then the rule: “To the law and to the testimony: if they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them” (Isaiah 8:20)."),
("p", "The volume reads Isaiah 8:20 as the same rule as Deuteronomy 13, said by a prophet to his own generation. The measure is “the law and the testimony,” a fixed word already given. The verdict on a voice that fails it is not that it is mistaken or weak. It is that “there is no light in them.” The ground is the word given through Moses, and a later voice is held to it. The prophets do not ask Israel to believe them because they say so; they send Israel back to what the LORD has already said and ask whether the new word agrees."),
("p", "This is why the loyalty test works when a sign cannot. A sign can be borrowed or imitated, and Deuteronomy 13 allows that a sign may even come to pass. A fixed word cannot be imitated by a stranger without contradicting itself. The test is therefore the surest the prophets give for a day when feeling runs high and the crowd wants a word. It was the test Israel could apply without waiting for an outcome."),
("sub", "3.7 The prophets in Babylon"),
("p", "Jeremiah 29 shows the test applied to a second audience. The letter is written to the exiles, who had their own prophets and diviners, and the instruction is direct. “Let not your prophets and your diviners, that be in the midst of you, deceive you, neither hearken to your dreams which ye cause to be dreamed. For they prophesy falsely unto you in my name: I have not sent them, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 29:8, 9). Two tests stand together in one verse. The prophets speak “in my name,” which is the claim of sending, and the LORD answers “I have not sent them,” which is the test of source."),
("p", "Then the LORD names two men, Ahab the son of Kolaiah and Zedekiah the son of Maaseiah, “which prophesy a lie unto you in my name” (Jeremiah 29:21). The charge adds a second element to the lie. “They have committed villany in Israel, and have committed adultery with their neighbours’ wives, and have spoken lying words in my name, which I have not commanded them; even I know, and am a witness, saith the LORD” (Jeremiah 29:23). Here the text itself joins conduct to the false word. The volume holds the order it has argued since chapter 2: conduct is evidence, the LORD is the witness, and the charge that decides is that the words were “not commanded.”"),
("p", "The captives themselves took up a curse from the case: “The LORD make thee like Zedekiah and like Ahab, whom the king of Babylon roasted in the fire” (Jeremiah 29:22). A false word given in the LORD’s name was remembered by a nation and made into a proverb. It shows how seriously Israel took the test of loyalty and source, and how long the memory of a failed prophet could last."),
''', "Isaiah 8:20 sends every voice back “to the law and to the testimony”; Jeremiah 29 applies the same rule to the exiles’ prophets, and the LORD’s own witness settles the case.")

ADD['p4'] = ("4.7 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "4.7 The vision that tarries"),
("p", "The test of fulfilment can be misused, and the prophets say so. If a word that has not yet come to pass is called false, a patient hearer is punished for patience. Habakkuk is told the opposite. “Write the vision, and make it plain upon tables, that he may run that readeth it. For the vision is yet for an appointed time, but at the end it shall speak, and not lie: though it tarry, wait for it; because it will surely come, it will not tarry” (Habakkuk 2:2, 3). The LORD does not deny that the word has a long road. He says it has an appointed time, and that it speaks at the end."),
("p", "Ezekiel meets the opposite error in his own day. The people have a proverb: “The days are prolonged, and every vision faileth” (Ezekiel 12:22). It is the mockery of the hearer who has waited and decided the prophets were wrong. The LORD’s answer is to end the proverb: “I will make this proverb to cease … The days are at hand, and the effect of every vision” (Ezekiel 12:23). Then he states the principle: “For I am the LORD: I will speak, and the word that I shall speak shall come to pass; it shall be no more prolonged” (Ezekiel 12:25)."),
("p", "Put Habakkuk 2 beside Ezekiel 12 and the volume’s position on the test of fulfilment sharpens. The test in Deuteronomy 18:22 concerns a word spoken “in the name of the LORD” that “follow not, nor come to pass.” It is the failure of a specific, announced event that shows the LORD has not spoken. It does not license a hearer to declare a word dead because it has not yet come. Habakkuk says the vision tarries and Ezekiel says the mockers were wrong. A word with an appointed time that has not arrived has not failed."),
("p", "This is the reason the test carries the least weight in the moment. The hearer who must decide cannot yet see the outcome, and a word that is true may still be waiting. The tests of source and loyalty are what he can apply now. The test of fulfilment is how the LORD answers, in time, the question the hearer could not settle at the start. It confirms or condemns afterward; it does not tell the hearer what to do beforehand."),
''', "Habakkuk 2:2, 3 and Ezekiel 12:21–25 show that a word may tarry without failing; the test of fulfilment condemns a word that did not come to pass at its appointed time, and does not condemn patience.")

ADD['p5'] = ("5.6 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "5.6 Joel: “Who knoweth if he will return”"),
("p", "Joel gives the conditional word its plainest prayer. The LORD says through him, “Turn ye even to me with all your heart, and with fasting, and with weeping, and with mourning” (Joel 2:12). Then the appeal: “Rend your heart, and not your garments, and turn unto the LORD your God: for he is gracious and merciful, slow to anger, and of great kindness, and repenteth him of the evil” (Joel 2:13). The reason to turn is the LORD’s character, and the character includes that he “repenteth him of the evil.” That is the same verb the volume meets in Jeremiah 18 and Jonah 3."),
("p", "Joel then asks the question every honest hearer of a conditional word asks. “Who knoweth if he will return and repent, and leave a blessing behind him?” (Joel 2:14). The question has no promise attached. Joel does not say the LORD will relent, and the king of Nineveh had asked almost the same thing: “Who can tell if God will turn and repent?” (Jonah 3:9). In both places the turning is a real possibility and not a guarantee. The people are told to turn, and the outcome is left in the LORD’s hand."),
("p", "The volume argues from this that the conditional word does not dissolve into a promise or a threat. A hearer cannot treat an announced judgment as certain and decline to turn, and cannot treat it as empty and decline to be afraid. The word comes as a call. Nineveh’s king and Joel’s prophet both answer it with the same uncertainty and the same action. They do not wait to learn which way the LORD will go. They turn, because the LORD is “gracious and merciful, slow to anger,” and because the word they heard could still be turned."),
("p", "It also answers the charge that the prophets are inconsistent. A prophet who announces judgment and a prophet who calls for turning are not speaking against each other. The announcement is the reason to turn, and the turning is the reason the announcement may not come. Joel’s question and Jonah’s story together describe a word built to be answered."),
''', "Joel 2:12–14 and Jonah 3:9 show the conditional word as a call: the turning is real, the outcome is left to the LORD, and no hearer is excused from the turning.")

ADD['p6'] = ("6.8 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "6.8 Psalm 89: chastening and the oath in one passage"),
("p", "The strongest evidence that the conditional word and the sworn word are different things is a passage where both stand together. Psalm 89 reports the LORD’s oath to David, and in the same breath says what will happen if David’s children fail. “If his children forsake my law, and walk not in my judgments; if they break my statutes, and keep not my commandments; then will I visit their transgression with the rod, and their iniquity with stripes” (Psalm 89:30–32). That is a conditional word, with an “if” and a “then,” and a penalty for disobedience."),
("p", "The next verse does not cancel the oath. “Nevertheless my lovingkindness will I not utterly take from him, nor suffer my faithfulness to fail” (Psalm 89:33). The chastening falls, and the oath stands over it. Then the LORD says what no “if” can touch: “My covenant will I not break, nor alter the thing that is gone out of my lips. Once have I sworn by my holiness that I will not lie unto David. His seed shall endure for ever, and his throne as the sun before me” (Psalm 89:34–36)."),
("p", "The grammar of the passage teaches the volume’s distinction. The rod and the stripes are tied to “if,” and they fall on the children when the children fail. The covenant is tied to the oath and to the LORD’s holiness, and no failure of the children alters “the thing that is gone out of my lips.” The two words are not set against each other. They are placed one after the other so that the reader can see which is which. A hearer who read only verses 30 to 32 would think the promise conditional. A hearer who read only verses 34 to 36 would think there was no chastening. The psalm gives both and marks the difference."),
("p", "This settles a mistake the volume wishes to prevent. It is sometimes argued that because a word may fail through disobedience, no word of the LORD is certain, or that because the covenant is sure, no word is conditional. Psalm 89 refutes both. The prophets mark each word as the LORD gave it. The volume leaves the covenants themselves to the volumes that argue them, and takes from Psalm 89 only the pattern: the chastening is conditional, the oath is not, and the text says which is which."),
''', "Psalm 89:30–37 puts the conditional chastening and the sworn covenant side by side and marks the difference in a single passage.")

ADD['p7'] = ("7.5 What the volume takes", '''
("sub", "7.5 Testing is carried on, and the Law’s sentence is not"),
("p", "The New Testament does not leave the testing of prophecy behind. Paul tells the assembly at Corinth, “Let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge” (1 Corinthians 14:29). He adds that “the spirits of the prophets are subject to the prophets” (1 Corinthians 14:32). To the Thessalonians he writes, “Despise not prophesyings. Prove all things; hold fast that which is good” (1 Thessalonians 5:20, 21). The practice of judging what is said is plain. A word given in the assembly is not accepted because it was given; it is weighed."),
("p", "John gives the same instruction in his own words. “Beloved, believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God: because many false prophets are gone out into the world” (1 John 4:1). The letter then offers a confession as the mark: “Every spirit that confesseth that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh is of God” (1 John 4:2). This reading rests on the letter’s own audience markers and setting. The volume notes that John’s test is a test of content, set beside the tests of source and loyalty that the prophets gave, and it does not claim that the letter reproduces them."),
("p", "The Lord’s warning in Matthew 7 belongs in the same discussion but in its own setting. “Beware of false prophets, which come to you in sheep’s clothing, but inwardly they are ravening wolves” (Matthew 7:15). “Ye shall know them by their fruits” (Matthew 7:16). The fruit is conduct, and the volume has argued since chapter 2 that conduct is evidence of the source. The Lord also makes plain that prophesying in his name is not itself the proof: “Many will say to me in that day, Lord, Lord, have we not prophesied in thy name?” (Matthew 7:22). The reply is, “I never knew you: depart from me, ye that work iniquity” (Matthew 7:23). The saying is spoken to Israel in the Sermon on the Mount, and the volume reports it there."),
("p", "What the New Testament passages share with the prophets is the practice of testing. What they do not repeat is the sentence of Deuteronomy 13:5. That sentence belongs to Israel under the Law in the land, and the assembly at Corinth is not in that setting. The Body’s practice is to judge, to prove and to hold fast, and the volume does not carry the Law’s penalty into it. The tests the prophets gave are the roots of the practice, and the practice is the fruit; the sentence stays where Moses set it."),
''', "Paul, John and the Lord each require testing of a prophetic word; the practice is carried on, and the Law’s sentence under Deuteronomy 13:5 stays with Israel in the land.")

for key,(marker,newtxt,bullet) in ADD.items():
    s=open(key+'.py',encoding='utf-8').read()
    i=s.index('("sub", "%s' % marker)
    # renumber old last sub: bump number
    ch=marker.split('.')[0]; n=int(marker.split('.')[1].split()[0])+1
    old_rest=s[i:]
    old_rest=old_rest.replace('("sub", "%s.%d What the volume takes' % (ch,n-1), '("sub", "%s.%d What the volume takes' % (ch,n),1)
    # fix the new section's numbering: they were authored with number n-1; new numbering uses n-1 for it already
    new = newtxt.lstrip('\n')
    # bullet
    old_rest=re.sub(r'(\("bul", \[\n(?:.*\n)*?)(  \]\),\n\])\s*$', lambda m: m.group(1)+'    "%s",\n' % bullet.replace('"','\\"') + m.group(2)+'\n', old_rest)
    s=s[:i]+new+old_rest
    open(key+'.py','w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)
    print(key,'ok')
